# on-edit-block — rules
#
# Each rule maps a gitignore-style pattern to the file(s) the agent must have
# read this session before it may Write/Edit/MultiEdit a matching path.
# Lines that don't parse are reported (and ignored) instead of failing
# silently.
#
#   <pattern> -> <file>[, <file>...]   require reading <file>(s) first
#   !<pattern>                         never require for these paths
#
# Patterns are gitignore-style, matched against the project-relative path:
#
#   *    any run of characters within one path segment
#   ?    a single character within one path segment
#   **   any number of path segments (a/**/b also matches a/b)
#
# A pattern containing a slash is anchored at the project root; one without
# a slash matches the file's basename at any depth. A trailing slash targets
# a directory and everything under it.
#
# Rules apply top to bottom: a matching "->" line adds its file(s) to the
# requirement; a matching "!" line clears everything matched so far, and
# later lines can add again. Required paths are relative to the project root.
#
# How blocking works: an edit of a matching path is denied with the required
# file's content inlined in the denial message (or, if too large, an
# instruction to Read it). A file blocks at most once per session: afterwards
# it counts as read — whether because the transcript shows a Read/Bash-cat
# of it, or because the hook already injected it.
#
# Examples:
#
#   server/**/models/**          -> docs/coding-standards/PYTHON-PREFERENCES.md
#   desktop/client/**/models/**  -> docs/coding-standards/PYTHON-PREFERENCES.md
#   **/*.sql                     -> docs/DB-CONVENTIONS.md, docs/GLOSSARY.md
#   !vendor/**
